.TH CHASHMAP 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CHASHMAP \- overview of the chashmap hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A chmap maps keys to values, and an insert, a lookup and a delete each
take O(1) time on average. At creation, the map selects one of two
implementation strategies:
  - Open addressing, when the key and the value are both integral types
    of eight bytes or less (long double is not included).
  - Separate chaining, for all other combinations, and also for a map
    that has a custom key equality procedure.
Both strategies give the same API. chmap_create_full(3) describes the
selection, the hashing and the resistance to hash flooding.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, for example chmap_construct(3),
    chmap_insert(3), chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3) and chmap_remove(3),
    keep the key type and the value type with the handle. On a hard
    error, they stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
  - The raw functions, chmap_create_full(3), chmap_insert_elem(3),
    chmap_get_elem_ref(3), chmap_get_elem_copy(3) and
    chmap_delete_elem(3), take each key and each value as a cmap_pair
    and return a ccol_retval_t.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP OF KEYS AND VALUES"
.nf
The map keeps a copy of each key and each value: an insert copies the
bytes of the key and of the value into storage that the map owns, and
an update frees the old value.

A char * key or value is a NUL-terminated string, and the map copies
the contents of the string together with its terminator. The caller
keeps its own string and can change or free it as soon as the insert
returns, without any effect on the map.

Some calls give back a string pointer: chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3),
chmap_get_elem_ref(3) and an iterator. That pointer points into the
storage of the map, so do not free it. It is valid until the next
insert, delete, reset or resize of the map, or until the map is
destroyed. To replace a stored string, insert the key again.

The map keeps a value of any other pointer type, for example a
struct point *, as the pointer itself: it does not copy, follow or free
the object at that address. The caller owns that object and must free
it before or during the destroy of the map; chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3)
runs a destructor on each value during the destroy.
.fi
.SH "REFERENCES INTO THE MAP"
.nf
A pointer from chmap_get_ptr(3) or chmap_get_elem_ref(3) is valid until
the map changes, which an insert, a delete, a reset and a resize do. A
lookup does not make a pointer invalid, so the caller can keep pointers
to many values at the same time. Each such pointer is aligned for the
value type, with both strategies.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A map has no internal lock. A caller can share one map between threads,
but must then serialize each call that changes the map against all
other calls on that map.
.fi
